        "y": 189,
        "name": "pBody1",
        "fill": "#505A67",
        "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
        "width": 430,
        "content": "The most important part of any quantum computer are its quantum bits, or qubits. IonQ’s qubits are ionized ytterbium atoms, a silvery rare-earth metal. Each ytterbium atom is perfectly identical to every other ytterbium atom in the universe.",
        "lineHeight": 1.45,
        "fontFamily": "Inter",
        "fontSize": 18,
        "fontWeight": "500"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "id": "MOMSC",
        "x": 22,
        "y": 347,
        "name": "pBody2",
        "fill": "#505A67",
        "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
        "width": 430,
        "content": "Moreover, once prepared in a particular stable quantum state, they can remain in that state for very long periods of time — they’re so consistent they’re used in one of the most accurate atomic clocks ever built.",
        "lineHeight": 1.45,
        "fontFamily": "Inter",
        "fontSize": 18,
        "fontWeight": "500"
